[observation.6.11.8]
task = "6.11"
file = "agent/crates/layerx-wire/tests/structures.rs"
symbol = "unknown_version_activity_and_field_are_deterministic_rejections protocol_and_canonical_network_bytes_cannot_reuse_signatures module_and_guest_versions_are_independent"
observed = "The three retained protocol-version assertions were written before req.14 and task 6.11 added explicit protocol version 3, and each encoded 3 as the first unsupported version rather than an unsupported version as such. Source authority: layerx_wire::limits::protocol_version_supported accepts 1, 2 and 3 with MAX_PROTOCOL_VERSION = STATE_COMMITMENT_PROTOCOL_VERSION = 3, layerx_crypto::SignatureMessage::new delegates to that predicate, and layerx_proof::receipt::supported_protocol_version delegates to protocol_version_uses_occupancy which accepts 2 and 3. Reconciled without removing any rejection: wire now proves a protocol-4 envelope is VersionUnsupported at offset 0, an envelope 3 with field 1 still at the corpus value 1 is VersionUnsupported at offset 8, and an activity carrying 3 in both places decodes with protocol_version 3 and re-encodes byte-identically; the unknown-activity and unknown-field rejections are unchanged. Crypto proves SignatureMessage accepts 1, 2 and 3 and refuses 0 and 4 as VersionUnsupported with the zero-network and bad-signature checks unchanged. Proof proves supported_protocol_version refuses 0, 1 and 4 and accepts 2 and 3, and adds the selected-version module table check: protocol 3 accepts exactly Programs module 4 for outcome and account-state receipts, protocol 2 refuses module 4 and keeps its 1..3 and 2..3 sets. Gates: cargo test --offline -p layerx-wire (6 suites ok, /tmp/layerx-conflicts-test-wire.log), -p layerx-crypto (8 suites ok, /tmp/layerx-conflicts-test-crypto.log), -p layerx-proof (9 suites ok, /tmp/layerx-conflicts-test-proof.log), -p layerx-types (9 suites ok, /tmp/layerx-conflicts-test-types.log). --locked could not be used because an uncommitted layerx-client Cargo.toml dependency change in the working tree makes agent/Cargo.lock stale; Cargo.lock was restored to HEAD after the runs."
assumption = "The corpus template activity is protocol 1; the positive protocol-3 vector is derived from it by setting both version fields, which exercises the identical field layout decode_internal uses for every version. No published corpus vector was changed."
severity = "note"

[observation.6.11.9]
task = "6.11"
file = "agent/crates/layerx-crypto/src/remote.rs"
symbol = "handshake_refusal sign_once"
observed = "Root cause of the intermittent RemoteTimeout-versus-RemoteAuthentication result in tests/remote.rs refusal_timeout_and_malformed_response_remain_distinct: sign_once mapped every openssl HandshakeError to SignRefusal::Authentication, including a socket read or write deadline expiring during the TLS handshake. With the 50 ms deadline that case sets, an RSA-2048 handshake on a loaded host can exceed the deadline before the request is written, so a deadline expiry was reported as an authentication refusal, contradicting the documented SignRefusal::Timeout taxonomy (connect, handshake, write or read exceeded the deadline). The transport now inspects HandshakeError::Failure and WouldBlock mid-handshake I/O errors and maps ErrorKind::TimedOut or WouldBlock to Timeout; every other handshake failure, including SetupFailure and certificate or name verification, remains Authentication. Added handshake_exceeding_the_deadline_is_a_timeout_not_an_authentication_refusal, a server that accepts TCP and never speaks TLS; it fails on the previous transport with Err(RemoteAuthentication) (/tmp/layerx-conflicts-test-crypto-remote-before-fix.log) and passes on the repaired one. Three consecutive runs of the remote suite pass 7/7 (/tmp/layerx-conflicts-test-crypto-remote-repeat.log)."
assumption = "The server-identity mismatch case still observes zero request attempts and RemoteAuthentication, so no authentication refusal was reclassified."
severity = "note"

[observation.6.11.10]
task = "6.11"
file = "agent/crates/layerx-wire/src/receipt.rs"
symbol = "ProtocolReceipt::from_sequence agent-lint"
observed = "The agent workspace strict Clippy gate (make agent-lint: cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings with workspace-level deny of clippy::all and clippy::pedantic and avoid-breaking-exported-api = false) remains red on committed code that predates this work. layerx-types/src/intent.rs ProgramLegacyCallResponse::new now carries its real # Errors section and layerx-types passes strict Clippy alone (/tmp/layerx-conflicts-clippy-types-strict.log). Test-local lints in layerx-wire (similar_names and format_collect in the program_outcome_vectors module, expect_used in tests/structures.rs), layerx-crypto (expect_used in tests/verify.rs) and layerx-proof (redundant_closure_for_method_calls and expect_used in tests/receipt.rs) were repaired without changing any assertion. Still red at HEAD and left intact: layerx-wire/src/receipt.rs:313 pub const fn from_sequence(&self) trips wrong_self_convention because the accessor mirrors the canonical 402LXP receipt field name from_sequence (spec/layerx-protocol/requirements.md) and is called by platform/emulator/src/main.rs:2523 and platform/emulator/tests/gateway.rs:541; layerx-proof/src/program.rs:265 and :427 too_many_lines, :566 too_many_arguments, src/receipt.rs:20 manual_range_patterns, :423 and :487 missing_errors_doc, src/state.rs:21 struct_excessive_bools. Because Clippy lints workspace-member dependencies, the wire lint alone stops the gate for every dependent crate. Strict run: /tmp/layerx-conflicts-clippy-strict.log (exit 101). Diagnostic run allowing exactly those six pre-existing lint names and nothing else passes for wire, crypto, proof and types (/tmp/layerx-conflicts-clippy-diagnostic-preexisting-allowed.log, exit 0), so no other lint remains in the touched crates."
